  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1123 PM CDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\nAn upper-level high continues to take up nearly the CONUS to make up \nthe most of the synoptic pattern. The only other notable aspect \ncontinues to be a very deep low pressure system over the Pacific \nNorthwest. Nearer to the surface, weak low pressure continues to \npersist across SW Kansas. Weak storms have festered for the last few \nhours with sub-severe storms as forecast. In regards to ongoing \nheadlines, a Heat Advisory remains in effect for the Highway 183 \ncorridor and eastward until 8 PM Sunday.\n\nSaturday will continue the pattern of very hot temperatures and \nstorm potential out west. Highs are forecast around 100 degrees with \napparent temperatures approaching 105 degrees in the eastern zones. \nThe focus on the forecast tomorrow is the storm chances. It is \nexpected to represent Friday's storms very similarly. Around 0Z, \nstorms are expected to develop and move out of Colorado. A marginal \nrisk is in effect for the far northwest counties. It is expected \nthat most, if not all of the storms, remain sub-severe similar to \nFriday. Forecast NAM soundings have held firm progging 1200 J/kg \nCAPE, 20 KTs shear, and low level lapse rates of 10 C/km. It seems \nunlikely that storms will be able to organize and mature to become \nsevere, but a few severe winds gusts remain possible. \n\nSunday through Tuesday will keep the heat, but with a near zero \nchance for storms (<5% via ensembles). The days are forecast to be a \ndegree or two cooler than Friday/Saturday, but highs are still \nforecast in the upper 90s and tangibly the difference won't be \nnoticed. \n\nTuesday night/Wednesday morning will be the next good chance for \nprecipitation. That said, ensembles have been trending drier and \nfarther northwest, now only covering the northwest portion of the \nCWA up to around a 25% chance for storms. Early indication is that \nthese storms will remain sub-severe, but that is subject to change. \nAfter Tuesday, the pattern of hot highs and small storm chances out \nwest continues through the remainder of the forecast period. Fire \nweather conditions may remain elevated with a strong south wind, \nespecially sunday, of up to 20 mph and gusts up to 25-30. However, \nrelative humidities will remain well above 20% and stay away from \nany fire weather headline criteria. With that in mind, traditional \nfire weather precautions are always advised.",
